# No deficiency, adversarial false-positive test. The narrative intentionally does not account for the ultimate disposition of every dollar, does not repeat the SAR filing date, and does not provide unnecessary detail about routine account activity. None of those omissions is material to understanding the suspicious activity.

River City Bank is filing this SAR on customer Eric Dalton, age 33, of Cincinnati, Ohio, regarding suspected money mule activity and possible movement of fraud proceeds through personal checking account 51073842. Dalton opened the account on October 6, 2025.

Between July 9, 2026, and August 17, 2026, Dalton received eight incoming ACH credits totaling $95,600 from eight individuals located in Ohio, Michigan, Illinois, Pennsylvania, and Kentucky. Individual credits ranged from $9,600 to $14,200. Payment descriptions included “services,” “invoice,” “consulting,” and “project.”

The incoming credits were:

July 9: $11,800 from Amanda Lewis.
July 15: $12,400 from Gregory Price.
July 22: $9,600 from Monica Reed.
July 28: $14,200 from Thomas Kelly.
August 2: $10,500 from Rachel Morgan.
August 7: $13,100 from Steven Brooks.
August 12: $12,700 from Michelle Carter.
August 17: $11,300 from Kevin Foster.

River City Bank identified no apparent family, employment, or business relationship between Dalton and the eight senders through available customer information, account history, and public-record research.

Between July 11 and August 18, 2026, Dalton initiated five outgoing transfers totaling $82,100. Three transfers totaling $50,800 were sent to two cryptocurrency exchanges, and two domestic wires totaling $31,300 were sent to Northstar Digital Ventures LLC at a financial institution in New York.

The outgoing transactions were:

July 11: $16,500 to a cryptocurrency exchange.
July 24: $17,800 to Northstar Digital Ventures LLC.
July 30: $15,400 to a cryptocurrency exchange.
August 9: $18,900 to a cryptocurrency exchange.
August 18: $13,500 to Northstar Digital Ventures LLC.

Each outgoing transaction occurred within three business days of one or more incoming ACH credits.

Historical account review from October 2025 through June 2026 showed no comparable pattern of third-party ACH credits, outgoing wires, or transfers to cryptocurrency exchanges. The account previously showed primarily payroll deposits, ordinary consumer purchases, rent, utilities, and personal transfers.

On August 5, 2026, River City Bank contacted Dalton regarding the recent increase in incoming payments. Dalton stated that he had begun performing remote administrative work for an individual he met through an online employment advertisement. Dalton said his responsibilities included receiving payments from customers and forwarding funds to businesses and cryptocurrency accounts designated by his employer.

Dalton stated that he was instructed to retain approximately 10 percent of the money he received as compensation. He did not provide an employment agreement, tax documentation, company website, physical business address, or full legal name for the individual directing the transactions.

Dalton provided screenshots of messages containing payment instructions. The messages directed Dalton to expect specific incoming payments and, after receipt, instructed him where to send portions of the funds. The messages also instructed Dalton to confirm when each transfer was completed.

On August 14, 2026, River City Bank received a fraud inquiry from the financial institution that originated the July 28 $14,200 ACH credit from Thomas Kelly. The originating institution advised that its customer reported being deceived into sending funds in connection with a fraudulent employment scheme. River City Bank has not independently verified the originating customer’s allegation and has not received fraud notifications concerning the other incoming credits.

Public-record review identified Northstar Digital Ventures LLC as an active New York limited liability company formed in May 2026. Its listed address is associated with a commercial mail receiving location. River City Bank identified no apparent ownership or employment relationship between Dalton and Northstar Digital Ventures LLC.

River City Bank considers the activity suspicious because Dalton received substantial payments from multiple apparently unrelated individuals and rapidly forwarded a large portion of those funds to third-party business and cryptocurrency accounts at the direction of a person he met online. Dalton stated that he retained a percentage of the funds as compensation and was unable to provide documentation establishing a legitimate employment relationship.

The activity is consistent with suspected money mule activity and possible movement of fraud proceeds. The fraud inquiry associated with one incoming ACH credit further increases the bank’s concern regarding the source of the funds. River City Bank has not established that all incoming payments were fraudulent or that Dalton knowingly participated in the underlying fraud.

River City Bank restricted outgoing wire and cryptocurrency-related transfers on August 19, 2026 and placed the account under enhanced monitoring. The account remains open. Account statements, ACH records, wire and electronic transfer records, customer communications, customer due diligence information, public-record research, and correspondence from the originating financial institution are available upon request.

